---
import EnterpriseDetail from '../../components/enterprise/EnterpriseDetail.astro';
import { SUPPORT_EMAIL } from '../../data/enterprise';
---

<EnterpriseDetail
  slug="open-source"
  seoTitle="Open Source and Auditable | SAM Enterprise"
  seoDescription="SAM is AGPL-3.0 open source. Every component is in one public repository, so your security team can read the code that handles your credentials and machines."
  title="Read the code that runs your agents"
  subtitle="Every part of SAM is in one public repository under AGPL-3.0. There is no closed companion service and no enterprise edition."
>
  <h2>What is in the repository</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The Cloudflare Worker API and its Durable Objects</li>
    <li>The web app</li>
    <li>The Go VM agent that runs on each machine</li>
    <li>Cloud-init templates and provider integrations</li>
    <li>Deployment, installer and update workflows</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/raphaeltm/simple-agent-manager" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Browse the repository on GitHub</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/architecture/security/">security architecture</a> documents how credentials are encrypted,
    how tokens are issued and how workspaces are isolated, citing the code that does each.
  </p>

  <h2>The AGPL-3.0 licence</h2>
  <p>
    You can run, study, change and redistribute SAM. If you modify it and let other people use your modified
    version over a network, the licence requires you to offer them its source code. That obligation applies to
    modified versions.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your organization needs different licence terms, commercial licensing is available. Email
    <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Fork if you need to</h2>
  <p>
    If SAM's direction stops matching yours, you can fork it and keep your installation running on your own
    schedule. Self-hosted installations update by pulling a release into your fork, so you choose when to take
    changes.
  </p>
</EnterpriseDetail>
